@@ -0,0 +1,112 @@
+# Claude and native Codex coordinators
+
+Both coordinators use the same Forge commands, worker model policy, approval
+gates and task evidence. Claude Code uses its existing Codex companion plugin.
+Codex CLI/Desktop launches registered native Codex workers without that plugin.
+`FORGE_COORDINATOR=claude|codex` explicitly selects a route; otherwise Forge
+detects the current Codex session and retains the legacy Claude route outside it.
+
+## Launch and recovery
+
+Run `./forge doctor --coordinator codex` to check CLI dependencies and trusted
+hook coverage. This checks the selected CLI executable; direct Desktop support
+also requires live evidence from Desktop's actual runtime. Native writes require the installed Codex runtime to load the
+checkout's matching hooks; missing, disabled, untrusted or divergent inherited
+hooks refuse the launch. Existing client scaffolding and upgrades install both
+adapters and the shared implementation.
+
+Use `./forge explore --prompt-file <brief>` for read-only Terra/high exploration,
+or add `--purpose validate` for Sol/xhigh validation. `--background` returns after
+protected process registration. `./forge delegate` retains the approved task,
+stage, scope and model gates and chooses the coordinator's worker transport.
+The native launcher registers process identity before releasing the prompt and
+keeps hooks enabled; only the live registered worker receives its bounded write
+grant. A zero process exit without a completed native turn does not pass.
+
+`./forge codex status` displays native jobs alongside existing companion jobs.
+`./forge codex cancel <launch-id>` stops the registered native process and its
+observed descendants after revoking its write admission. Failed cleanup keeps
+admission revoked and permits a cleanup retry. `./forge codex resume <launch-id> --prompt-file <brief>`
+continues a terminal read-only session with its recorded identity. Write work
+must return through `./forge delegate` and the current gates. Cancellation and
+worker completion share one terminal publication so retries remain usable.
+
+## Questions and planning
+
+Use Codex's built-in synchronous `request_user_input` for structured planning
+questions, the equivalent of Claude Code's `AskUserQuestion`. Keep questions in
+the user's current conversation. Forge plan authoring remains mode-agnostic
+under Decision 0050.
+
+For a Codex coordinator, when the user requests Plan mode or the current
+runtime explicitly requires it for the next action, check the actual session
+mode. If a manual switch is needed, explain the specific reason and say:
+
+> Switch this task to **Plan mode** in Codex, then send “continue.”
+
+Keep the pending step and draft in the same conversation. On continuation,
+verify Plan mode from the runtime's current instructions before resuming that
+step with the available native question tool. A “continue” reply or Forge's
+`planning` phase does not prove a mode change or approve a plan. If the runtime
+still reports another mode, say so; if mode information is unavailable, report
+that verification is unavailable. Continue independent work while the step
+waits. Ordinary planning and supported questions in Default mode need no
+switch. A missing question tool alone does not establish a Plan-mode
+requirement; follow the runtime's actual tool availability and restrictions.
+This manual-switch instruction applies only to the Codex coordinator.
+
+| Concept | Claude Code | Codex |
+|---|---|---|
+| Planning mode | Plan mode | Plan mode |
+| Structured question tool | `AskUserQuestion` | `request_user_input` |
+| New question producer | `claude-code:ask-user-question` | `codex:request-user-input` |
+
+Question provenance identifies the runtime and tool. The mode is a separate
+field copied from the actual event when supplied; a question asked in Default
+mode is not labeled as a Plan-mode event. The old `claude-code:plan-mode`
+question producer remains accepted for historical records. Plan authoring and
+its historical markers are separate from question provenance.
+
+Both tools feed `factory/scripts/post_tool_use.py`. Codex answers are keyed by
+question ID and contain an answer array. Only one submitted answer matching an
+offered option can become a certified round. Missing, canceled, multiple or
+free-text answers require clarification before certification. The recorder
+does not persist private free-text replies. Async question issuance is not an
+answer and cannot produce a certified round.
+
+New event-bearing records require `event_id` and `question_id` in each grill
+claim, copied from the actual completed record. These bind repeated wording to
+one answered event in either runtime. Codex question identity is its supplied
+input id; Claude question identity is its actual response-map key. Historical
+`claude-code:plan-mode` records without event identity retain their matching rules.
+
+The shared PreToolUse handler applies the existing interruption gate to Claude
+questions and both native question tools. Planning questions remain available;
+an open task requires the existing recorded escalation. The six grill gates
+retain their round floors, convergence, artifact binding and one-time use.
+
+## Readiness and bootstrap
+
+FORGE-COORD-1 begins with a bounded native question change explicitly requested
+by the user on 2026-09-07. The prior Claude bootstrap was canceled. This change
+does not itself accept the proposed decision, approve a plan, pass a grill or
+provide delegated-task proof. All existing platforms remain in scope, as the
+user selected through a real native structured question.
+
+Before certifying a native round, verify that the selected CLI/Desktop runtime
+loads this checkout's trusted hooks and emits the real completed synchronous
+question event with session and tool-call identity. A visible answer alone
+does not prove hook delivery. Test fixtures establish parsing and refusal
+behavior; they are not human provenance. If no real ledger event exists, keep
+the gate unmet and diagnose native hook support without launching Claude.
+
+Native launch, admission, setup and recovery are implemented with regression
+coverage in FORGE-COORD-1. That coverage does not certify live write admission or
+question provenance in a Desktop task still using older hooks. Live activation
+must load the matching trusted adapter before those checks can pass. The existing
+Codex worker model policy and review procedure remain in force.
